course: everyday
lesson: 308
title: Coins, pocket money, saving, spending wisely and planning time
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Money comes from work. Adults earn a salary by doing a job, and that money pays for the home, food and bills. A bank card does not make money; it only spends money that someone has already earned.
explain: Knowing your coins lets you pay and check your change. In the USA a penny is 1 cent, a nickel 5 cents, a dime 10 cents and a quarter 25 cents. In the UK coins go from 1p to £2; Australia, Canada and New Zealand use cents and dollar coins, and Ireland uses euro cents and euro coins.
explain: Small amounts add up: a girl in Texas who puts two dollars a week in her piggy bank and does not keep opening it has over one hundred dollars after a year (a year has 52 weeks, so that is 104 dollars).
explain: Before paying, round each price and add them in your head, so you know if you have enough before you reach the till. And when planning your day, time a job once to see how long it really takes, because most people think jobs are quicker than they are.
fact: In the USA a penny is 1 cent, a nickel 5 cents, a dime 10 cents and a quarter 25 cents; 100 cents make one dollar.
fact: UK coins go from 1p to £2; Australia, Canada and New Zealand use cents and dollars, and Ireland uses euro cents and euros.
fact: Keep pocket money in a safe place, such as a money box or purse, and keep track of how much you have so you know what you can afford.
fact: Putting money in a piggy bank regularly and not opening it lets small amounts add up over time.
fact: Cheap things that break quickly can cost more in the end than something that lasts.
fact: To estimate a total, round each price and add the rounded numbers in your head.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand summer is December to February, so Christmas is in summer; the seasons are opposite in the southern hemisphere.
fact: Most people think jobs take less time than they really do; timing a job once shows how long it takes.
fact: Doing a chore properly builds trust and teaches you useful skills.
fact: Adults earn money by working, and that money pays for the home, food and bills; a card machine does not make money.
fact: When invited to a party, reply by the date asked so the host knows how many children are coming.
exercise: In the USA, how much is a dime worth? | 10 cents | 5 cents | 25 cents | 1 cent
exercise: A boy in Florida has two quarters and one nickel. How much money does he have? | 55 cents | 30 cents | 51 cents | 60 cents
exercise: Why is it useful to know the coins of your country? | So you can pay and check your change | So you can tell which coins are the shiniest | Because shops do not accept notes from children | Because coins are worth more than notes
exercise: A girl gets an allowance every Saturday. What is a sensible thing to do with it? | Keep it somewhere safe and keep track of how much she has | Carry all of it loose in her coat pocket so it is always there when she needs it | Spend it all on the same day so she cannot lose it | Never count it so it feels like more
exercise: A boy wants to save for a new football. Which plan works best? | Put some money in his money box every week and leave it there | Open the money box each day to check on it | Save only when he has a large amount left over | Borrow the money and pay it back one day
exercise: A toy car costs $3 but breaks in a week, and a sturdier one costs $8 and lasts for years. Which is true? | Buying the cheap one again and again can cost more in the end | The cheapest toy is always the best value | The price of a toy tells you nothing about it | Toys that break quickly are better because they are new
exercise: In a shop you pick items costing $1.95, $3.10 and $4.90. About how much will they cost together? | About $10 | About $8 | About $12 | About $15
exercise: Why estimate the total in your head before you reach the till? | To know whether you have enough money | To make the shop give you a discount | Because the till cannot add up prices | To make the queue move more slowly
exercise: When is summer in New Zealand? | December to February | June to August | March to May | September to November
exercise: Why can a girl in Melbourne have a picnic on the beach on Christmas Day? | Christmas falls in summer in the southern hemisphere | Australia is much closer to the Sun in December than Europe is | Christmas in Australia is held in July | It never gets cold anywhere in Australia
exercise: You think tidying your room takes five minutes, but you are often late. What should you do? | Time yourself once to see how long it really takes | Tidy faster so it fits into five minutes | Leave the tidying until the weekend | Ask someone else to guess the time for you
exercise: A boy dries the dishes badly so he will not be asked again. What should he do instead? | Do the job properly, which builds trust and skill | Do it even worse next time, so his parents give up asking him for good | Refuse to help with any chore at home | Hide the dishes he did not want to dry
exercise: Where does the money for a family's home, food and bills come from? | From the work the adults do to earn a salary | From the card machine at the shop | From the bank, which gives money to everyone free | From the change that comes back after shopping
exercise: You are invited to a birthday party and the card says «Please reply by Friday.» What do you do? | Reply by Friday saying whether you can come | Just turn up on the day without replying | Reply only if you decide not to go | Wait until the day after the party to reply
exercise: Why does the party host want replies by a certain date? | To know how many children are coming | To check that everyone can read | To choose which guests get a present | Because replying late is against the law
test: A girl in New York pays for a 70-cent snack with a one-dollar bill. How much change should she get? | 30 cents, for example one quarter and one nickel | 70 cents, for example two quarters and two dimes | 25 cents, for example one quarter | 3 cents, for example three pennies
test: A boy keeps his pocket money in a jar but never counts it. What problem might he have? | He will not know what he can afford | His money will slowly be worth less | The jar will fill up too quickly | He will always have enough for anything
test: Lily saves one dollar every week and never opens her money box. What will she have after a year? | About 52 dollars | About 12 dollars | About 7 dollars | About 365 dollars
test: A boy buys a cheap umbrella every month because each one breaks in the wind. What would be a wiser choice? | Pay a little more once for an umbrella that lasts | Keep buying the cheapest one every month | Never buy an umbrella and stay at home when it rains | Buy two cheap umbrellas at once
test: You have £5. You want items costing £1.90, £2.10 and £1.95. Which is the quickest sensible check? | Round to £2 each, about £6, so you do not have enough | Hand over the £5 and hope it is enough | Ask the cashier to guess the total for you before you hand it over | Buy them all and pay the rest next week
test: A cousin in Canada and a cousin in Australia are both writing about their July. Which is true? | It is summer in Canada and winter in Australia | It is summer in both countries | It is winter in both countries | It is spring in Canada and autumn in Australia
test: Which is true about how long homework will take? | People usually guess it will be quicker than it really is | People usually guess it will be much slower than it is | Everybody always guesses the time exactly right | Homework always takes exactly half an hour
test: A girl is paid a little for washing the family car and does it carefully. What does she gain besides the money? | Her family trusts her more and she learns a useful skill | Nothing really, because in the end only the money she is paid matters | She will never have to do another chore | She can now drive the car herself
test: A little boy thinks the card machine in a shop makes money. What would you explain? | The card only spends money that adults earned by working | The machine makes new money every time it beeps | Shops give money to anyone with a card | Money appears in the bank when it is needed
test: You forgot to reply to a party invitation and the date has passed. What is the best thing to do now? | Tell the host straight away whether you can come | Turn up on the day without saying anything, since the host will make room | Ignore it because it is too late anyway | Ask a friend to go instead of you
test: got 2 quarters n a dime, how much is that | 60 cents | 35 cents | 50 cents | 70 cents
test: $3.95 n $2.10, rough total in ur head? | About $6 | About $5 | About $7 | About $8
test: auntie in sydney says xmas is in summer there. she's joking right? | No, seasons are opposite in the south | Yes, Christmas is always in winter, everywhere in the world | Yes, Australia has no summer | No, Australia has Christmas in July
test: cheap $2 ball broke again, 3rd one this month lol | Something that lasts can cost less in the end | Keep buying the cheap ones, they're cheaper every single time | Balls always break, it's normal | Buy five at once next time
explain: A boy in Ohio carried all his savings to the shops in an open pocket; it fell out, and months of saving were gone in an afternoon.
explain: A girl in Australia bought a cheap toy that broke the next day, then another, then another; in the end she had spent more than a good toy would have cost.
fact: Money that is lost in the street is very rarely found again.
fact: When you go out, take only the money you need and keep it in a zipped pocket or purse.
fact: Cheap things that break often can cost more over time than one good thing.
test: taking all my money to town in my back pocket | Take only what you need and zip it away | Take it all in your back pocket | Hold it in your hand all day | Hide it in your shoe
test: this toy is super cheap, buy 3 | Check if it will last; cheap can cost more | Yes, cheap always means good value, so you save money | Yes, buy as many as you can | Yes, quality doesn't matter
test: homework takes 10 min max, plenty of time | Plan more time; jobs usually take longer | 10 minutes is always enough | Start just before bed | Don't plan at all, just do it whenever you remember
test: did u hear a kid dropped a twenty out his pocket in town and "a seagull took it". lesson? | Keep money somewhere safe; money lost in the street is rarely found | Money always turns up again later, coz people hand it in to the police | Carry cash in your hand to watch it | Seagulls only steal food
test: my mate says his cousin "saved a million in his piggy bank". what's prob exaggerated | The million; but saving regularly does let small amounts add up | That piggy banks hold money | That saving adds up | That coins exist
test: kids say the girl who only gets small pocket money is "poor, her mum's got no money". pass it on? | No, it's unkind and not your business | Yes, she must be poor | Yes, and ask her mum | Yes, and laugh about it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the value of a United States dime? | Ten cents | Five cents | Twenty-five cents | One cent
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a cheap item that breaks quickly may prove costly. | It may cost more over time than one item that lasts | Cheap items are taxed more heavily | Cheap items cannot be returned | Cheap items lose value at once
exam: It is given that summer in Australia and New Zealand falls between December and February. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Christmas there falls in summer, since the seasons are reversed | Christmas there falls in winter, as it does in every other country | The seasons there are the same as in the UK | There is no summer there at all
exam: State the means by which adults obtain money to pay for home, food and bills. | By working | By using a card machine | By printing it at the bank | By asking the government each week
